ment from x=300 to 420. Looking at the right side of the image more carefully: there's a vertical line at x\u2248660 running from y\u2248240 down to y\u2248345, then a horizontal line extending from there to around x\u2248780 at the same y-level, and a longer vertical edge at x\u2248780 that spans most of the height.\n\nLet me trace through the actual structure more systematically. The image appears to contain several rectangular regions defined by these line segments: a vertical line on the left at x\u2248178 extending from the top down to around y\u2248348, a top horizontal spanning most of the width, then subdivisions created by additional verticals at x\u2248545 and x\u2248780, with horizontals at various y-positions creating the boundaries of distinct rectangular sections. horizontal y=730 from x=178 to 780 forms the bottom edge. There's also a horizontal line at y=530 spanning from x\u2248292 to 420 with a rounded left endpoint, meeting the vertical at x=420. That vertical line at x=420 extends from y=435 down to y=730.\n\nLooking more carefully at the middle section, there's a long horizontal line at y\u2248435 that runs continuously from x=420 all the way across to x=780, forming the upper boundary of that region. Now I'm trying to establish a connected path from the start position at (420,150) to the goal at (300,246). From the start, I can move left along the horizontal line at y=150 to reach x=300, but I need to verify if there's a vertical connector at x=300 that links down to the goal position\u2014looking at the image, that area appears dark with no visible vertical edge, and the goal line's left endpoint seems to terminate without a clear connection point. From the start, I can either go left along y=150 to x=178 (which hits a dead end going down), or right to x=545 (also a dead end). Both paths lead up to the top row at y=48. The top row extends right to x=790, then drops down at x=780 to y=143, creating a closed loop in the top-right rectangle. I'm 